# Coldwater: fishers and boat-builders on the shore of Glasswater, the cold clear lake under the mountains where the
# Long River rises, and where the High Road starts to climb. The Glass Lantern, the bakery, the fishmonger, the
# stables, the chapel and its reed crosses, the healer, the jetty, the reed beds, the mist, the winter ice, and
# whatever moves beneath it.

scene rg_ls_first_fish
when topic=lore region=coldwater
a : Threw the first fish back this morning, like always. | Never eat the first fish of the day. It belongs to the lake. | A carter off the High Road ate his first catch. Fell in the lake by noon.
b[fisher] : Glasswater gives the rest if you give back the first. Every boat knows it.
b[priest] : The Bright One owns the lake. Still, it does no harm.
b : Good habit. | So they say. | Served him right.
a : Always. | Always. | Ha.

scene rg_ls_lake_colour
when topic=smalltalk|lore region=coldwater
a : The lake's gone green as glass. Fish will be deep. | The water's dark today. Storm on the peaks. | Silver on the water at dawn. Best fishing all season.
b[fisher] : Read the colour, read the catch. My father's words.
b[old] : I've watched that water seventy years. It's never the same twice.
b : Deep and lazy. | Better stay in. | Get out there, then.
a : Lazy as me. | I will. | I'm going.

scene rg_ls_under_ice
when topic=lore region=coldwater season=winter|autumn
a : Old Heron swears something huge moved under the ice one winter. | When Glasswater freezes you can hear it groan all night. | My grandfather saw a shadow under the ice longer than a boat.
b[old] : The lake's deeper than anyone's rope. Who knows what's down there.
b[child] : Is it a monster? Will it eat me?
b : He's seen too many winters. | Just the ice settling. | A big pike, maybe.
a : Maybe. | Probably. | A very big pike.

scene rg_ls_boatwright
when topic=work region=coldwater a.job=crafter|apprentice|fisher
a : The new skiff's ribs went in today. | Pitch smell all over the boat shed. | Somebody holed their boat on the rocks off the point again.
b[crafter] : A good skiff's ribs are bent green and dried slow. Rush it, it leaks.
b : She'll be a beauty. | Smells like work. | Every month.
a : She will. | Good work. | Every month.

scene rg_ls_netmaker
when topic=work|smalltalk region=coldwater
a : The net loft looks like grey curtains hanging everywhere. | Ordered a new net. Three weeks' wait. | Pike tore through my net like it was spider silk.
b[crafter] : A net's knots, and knots are time. No hurrying knots.
b : It does. | Busy loft. | Big pike.
a : Lovely in the light. | Very busy. | Enormous.

scene rg_ls_shore_oven
when topic=smalltalk region=coldwater time=dawn
a : The bakery's bread is out before the first boats. | Smell of the bakery wakes the whole jetty. | Grabbed a hot loaf on the way to the boat.
b[baker] : Bread before dawn for the boats. Every day for thirty years.
b : Best smell there is. | Better than a bell. | Lucky you.
a : The best. | Much better. | Very lucky.

scene rg_ls_mere_chapel
when topic=lore region=coldwater
a : Hung a reed cross in the chapel for my brother's boat. | The chapel floor is laid with stones from the lake bed. | The chapel's beams are thick with reed crosses.
b[priest] : Every cross is a prayer for safe water.
b[old] : My mother laid one of those floor stones. Dived for it herself, in that cold.
b : May it keep him safe. | Lovely work. | Many prayers.
a : May it. | Very lovely. | Very many.

scene rg_ls_mist
when topic=smalltalk|weather region=coldwater weather=fog|overcast
a : Mist so thick I lost the jetty. | The mist comes in under the doors at the Glass Lantern. | Nobody rows out in this.
b[fisher] : Mist and still water. You row in circles and never know.
b : Wait till noon. | Every night. | Nobody sane.
a : I will. | Every night. | Nobody.

scene rg_ls_isle_courting
when topic=smalltalk region=coldwater band=friends|family|acquaintances
a : Young Reed hangs about the stables every Restday. Courting a carter. | Half the lake girls are sweet on the drovers off the High Road. | The carters come in for bread and stay for the dancing.
b[carter] : Lake folk are warmer than the High Road, that's sure.
b[old] : I courted a carter once. Married a fisher instead.
b : Sweet. | They always are. | Who could blame them?
a : Very sweet. | Always. | Nobody.

scene rg_ls_unclaimed
when topic=lore region=coldwater
a : They drew a body from the lake that nobody claimed. | The healer kept the drowned stranger's ring. Waiting for someone to ask. | They buried the stranger from the lake by the chapel.
b[priest] : We said the words over them. The Bright One knows their name.
b[guard] : No name, no town, no one asking. Strange business.
b : Who was it? | Nobody's asked? | Poor soul.
a : Nobody knows. | Nobody. | Poor soul.

scene rg_ls_drowned_oar
when topic=smalltalk region=coldwater place=tavern
a : The Glass Lantern's full of nets and lantern smoke tonight. | Boatmen's wagers on the race at the Lantern. | A fisher at the Lantern claims he caught a pike as long as a bench.
b[innkeeper] : Every fisher's pike gets a foot longer with every cup.
b[fisher] : I saw it. Long as a bench and twice as ugly.
b : Every night. | Who's winning? | And growing.
a : Every night. | Nobody yet. | By the cup.

scene rg_ls_reed_cutting
when topic=work region=coldwater a.job=laborer|farmer|crafter
a : Reed cutters were out at dawn in the shallows. | Thatching season. Every roof wants new reed. | Cut reed all day. My legs are numb from the water.
b[laborer] : Cut it low, bundle it tight. Wet reed rots, dry reed lasts.
b : Early work. | Busy time. | Dry them by the fire.
a : Very early. | Very busy. | I will.

scene rg_ls_hold_breath
when topic=smalltalk region=coldwater a.stage=young|adolescent
a : I held my breath to fifty under the jetty! | Watch me dive for a lake stone! | Old Pike can hold her breath longer than anyone. I'll beat her.
b[old] : Fifty? In water that cold? I did a hundred at your age. Come up for air, pup.
b : Show-off. | Be careful! | Maybe someday.
a : Watch me! | I will! | Someday!

scene rg_ls_whittled_boats
when topic=smalltalk region=coldwater
a : Old Willow whittled a little boat for every child on the shore. | The children race their whittled boats off the jetty. | Found a tiny wooden boat washed up. Somebody's lost it.
b[child] : Mine won! Mine's the fastest!
b : Kind wolf. | Every Restday. | Some pup's crying.
a : Very kind. | Every one. | Probably.

scene rg_ls_hook_wounds
when topic=lore|smalltalk region=coldwater
a : The healer pulled a hook from a fisher's paw this morning. | The healer's seen every kind of hook wound there is. | Lake-cold compresses. The healer swears by them.
b[healer] : Cut it out clean and wash it in cold water. Most heal.
b : Ouch. | I believe it. | They work.
a : Very ouch. | Every kind. | Mostly.

scene rg_ls_private_boat
when topic=lore region=coldwater
a : Nobody's allowed to touch old Heron's boat. Not even his kin. | That blue boat on the end has sat untouched ten years. | Why does nobody use that boat by the willows?
b[fisher] : That boat's for the one who didn't come back. It waits.
b[old] : Don't ask about that boat. Just don't.
b : Strange. | Ten years. | No idea.
a : Very strange. | Ten years. | I'll leave it.

scene rg_ls_trout
when topic=work region=coldwater a.job=fisher|cook|merchant
a : Trout were rising at dusk. Good sign. | Smoked trout sells best at the Glass Lantern. | Pike lines full this morning.
b[cook] : Trout in butter with lake herbs. Nothing better on any table.
b[merchant] : Trout sells itself. I just keep it cold.
b : Cast early tomorrow. | Always does. | Good catch.
a : I will. | Always. | Very good.

scene rg_ls_mirrormere_name
when topic=lore region=coldwater
a : On a still day Glasswater's clear as a window. You see stones forty feet down. | They say the lake shows you your future at midsummer dawn. | Glasswater gave the town everything. Fish, reed, stone.
b[old] : I looked in at midsummer once. Saw my own face. Very disappointing.
b[priest] : Superstition. Lovely, but superstition.
b : That's how it got the name. | So they say. | And takes what it wants.
a : It is. | So they say. | It does.

scene rg_ls_still_water_inn
when topic=smalltalk region=coldwater place=tavern|street
a : The Glass Lantern's low windows look right over the lake. | Smoked trout on every plate at the Glass Lantern. | Travellers pay double for a lake room at the Glass Lantern.
b[innkeeper] : A room over the water's worth double. Wake up to herons.
b : Best view in town. | Every plate. | Worth it.
a : The best. | Every one. | So they say.

scene rg_ls_jetty_repair
when topic=work region=coldwater a.job=crafter|laborer|fisher
a : The storm took two planks off the long jetty. | The jetty posts are rotting at the waterline. | Driving new piles for the jetty all week.
b[crafter] : Larch for piles. Pine rots in a season.
b : Mind your step. | They always do. | Hard work.
a : Always. | Always. | Very hard.

scene rg_ls_herons
when topic=smalltalk region=coldwater place=shore|street
a : Herons standing in the shallows like old priests. | A heron stole a fish right off my line. | Herons are the best fishers on the lake. Better than us.
b[child] : I tried to stand like a heron. I fell over.
b : Very still. | Cheeky bird. | True enough.
a : Very still. | Cheeky. | Too true.

scene rg_ls_isle_lights
when topic=lore region=coldwater time=dusk|night
a : Lanterns moving on the far shore tonight. | Somebody's lit a fire on the far shore. | A boat came over from the far shore at midnight.
b[guard] : Trappers down off the mountain, most likely. The watch has an eye on it.
b : Looking for something. | Camping, maybe. | What for?
a : Always. | Maybe. | Who knows?

scene rg_ls_weed
when topic=work|smalltalk region=coldwater season=summer
a : Snowmelt's still coming down. The water's cold as midwinter. | Swam in the shallows at midsummer. My teeth are still chattering. | Even in high summer Glasswater never warms.
b[fisher] : Cold water, fat trout. I'm not complaining.
b : Every summer. | Fool. | Never.
a : Every summer. | Fool. | Never.

scene rg_ls_drowning_season
when topic=lore region=coldwater band=friends|family|spouses
a : The lake took a swimmer at midsummer. The cold stops your heart. | They say Glasswater wants one wolf every summer. | I won't let my pups swim past the willows.
b[priest] : The lake takes the careless, not the chosen. Teach your young to swim.
b : Every year. | Grim saying. | Wise.
a : Every year. | Very grim. | Very wise.

scene rg_ls_mere_bandits
when topic=lore region=coldwater
a : Boats without lanterns on the north shore again. | Somebody's been taking fish from the traps at night. | The watch chased a boat across the lake last week.
b[guard] : We row the north shore at dawn. We'll find them.
b : Smugglers. | Thieves. | Did they catch it?
a : Probably. | Probably. | Not yet.

scene rg_ls_pike_story
when topic=lore|smalltalk region=coldwater
a : Old Mist's been chasing the same pike for twenty years. | They say there's a pike in the deep water old as the town. | Caught a pike with three hooks in its jaw already.
b[fisher] : That pike's broken more lines than I've cast.
b : He'll never catch it. | Ancient fish. | A survivor.
a : Never. | Ancient. | A true survivor.

scene rg_ls_eel_smoke
when topic=work|smalltalk region=coldwater
a : Smoked trout hanging in every chimney on the shore. | The smokers lit their fires before dawn. | Smoked trout's the only thing Coldwater sends down the High Road.
b[cook] : Trout smoked with alder wood. Nothing better in Ridgemere.
b : Smells like home. | Early work. | And the pike.
a : It does. | Very early. | And the pike.

scene rg_ls_boat_naming
when topic=lore region=coldwater
a : Never rename a boat. The lake won't know her. | They named the new skiff after the healer. | Every boat on Glasswater has a name and a ribbon.
b[fisher] : The ribbon's for the lake to find her in fog.
b : Never. | Good choice. | Old custom.
a : Never. | Very good. | Very old.

scene rg_ls_reed_crosses
when topic=lore|work region=coldwater
a : Weaving reed crosses for the chapel beams. | Every new boat gets a reed cross at its bow. | The children weave reed crosses on Restday.
b[priest] : A reed cross is a fisher's prayer, made with their own paws.
b : Fine work. | Old custom. | Lovely.
a : Thank you. | Very old. | Very lovely.

scene cw_long_river_rises
when topic=lore region=coldwater
a : All this water ends up in the fens, they say. Down the whole Long River. | Dropped a bottle where the Long River leaves the lake. Wonder where it'll fetch up. | The Long River starts right here, at the outfall. Just a stream you could jump.
b[old] : Cold up here, warm down there. Same water.
b[fisher] : Fish the outfall at dusk. Everything in the lake goes that way to feed.
b : A long way. | In the fens, maybe. | Hard to believe.
a : A very long way. | Maybe. | True, though.

scene cw_high_road_stables
when topic=smalltalk|work region=coldwater place=street|work|gate
a : The stables are full tonight. Everyone's waiting to go up the pass. | Carters change their horses here before the climb. | Lord Grayrock's party came through on the High Road, bound for the mines.
b[carter] : Fresh horses at Coldwater, or you don't make the pass.
b[guard] : Busy gate. Half of them I've never seen before.
b : Busy town. | Smart of them. | In this weather?
a : Very busy. | Very smart. | Rather him than me.
